<full--width--block data--page--block="">
  <full--width--area data--page--area="" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" style="outline: none;"><h1>Project Types</h1><div>Six types cover the main shapes a project can take. Each type comes with a workspace tuned to its vocabulary and workflow. Pick the one that matches how you actually think about the work.</div></full--width--area>
</full--width--block>
<image--text--block data--page--block="" src="/icons/chapter-image.svg" fit="contain" layout="image-left" size="small"><image--text--area data--page--area="" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" style="outline: none;"><h2>Hub</h2><div>A <marked-text>container project</marked-text> for an IP, organization, or anything that wraps multiple sub-projects. Focus is on <marked-text>lore, documentation, and glue content</marked-text> - the shared context that connects everything else.</div><div><br></div><div>Good for: a game universe with multiple titles, a studio organizing its productions, a creative IP with characters and world canon across formats.</div><div><br></div><div>Output is <marked-text>shared understanding</marked-text>, not a product. Nested sub-projects do the output work; the Hub holds the bigger picture.</div><div><br></div><div><a href="hub/index.page">Read more</a></div></image--text--area></image--text--block>
<image--text--block data--page--block="" src="/icons/chapter-image.svg" fit="contain" layout="image-left" size="small"><image--text--area data--page--area="" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" style="outline: none;"><h2>Software Dev</h2><div>A software project workspace. Comes with guides for <marked-text>code conventions, testing strategy, linting, and CI/CD</marked-text> - fill them in to match the stack and standards of the project.</div><div><br></div><div>Good for: any software product, library, tool, or service. Stack-agnostic by design - the guides are placeholders you and the agent fill out together during initialization.</div><div><br></div><div>Agent-first: once the guides are filled, any agent or contributor can read them and work without re-explaining the stack.</div><div><br></div><div><a href="software-dev/index.page">Read more</a></div></image--text--area></image--text--block>
<image--text--block data--page--block="" src="/icons/chapter-image.svg" fit="contain" layout="image-left" size="small"><image--text--area data--page--area="" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" style="outline: none;"><h2>Game Dev</h2><div>Organized around the <marked-text>game design document</marked-text> - mechanics, characters, scenes, asset lists, engine notes. <marked-text>Design-first</marked-text>: the GDD comes before the code.</div><div><br></div><div>Good for: Godot, Unity, Unreal projects, and engine-agnostic game jams.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>The workspace grows alongside the game - from early concept through production and polish.</div><div><br></div><div><a href="game-dev/index.page">Read more</a></div></image--text--area></image--text--block>
<image--text--block data--page--block="" src="/icons/chapter-image.svg" fit="contain" layout="image-left" size="small"><image--text--area data--page--area="" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" style="outline: none;"><h2>Production</h2><div>A workspace for theatre, film, live events, or recorded shows. Organized around logistics: <marked-text>scene breakdowns, prop and costume lists, cast and crew, schedules</marked-text>.</div><div><br></div><div>Good for: stage productions, short films, exhibitions, podcasts, recorded performances. Anything with a <marked-text>deadline</marked-text> and a list of things that need to exist on a specific day.</div><div><br></div><div>Boards track what needs to happen, by when, and who owns it.</div><div><br></div><div><a href="production/index.page">Read more</a></div></image--text--area></image--text--block>
<image--text--block data--page--block="" src="/icons/chapter-image.svg" fit="contain" layout="image-left" size="small"><image--text--area data--page--area="" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" style="outline: none;"><h2>Authoring</h2><div>A workspace for writing, worldbuilding, lore, or music composition. <marked-text>Wiki-like reference pages</marked-text> for characters, locations, timelines, and relationships.</div><div><br></div><div>Good for: novels, screenplays, tabletop RPG settings, game narratives, music albums with conceptual depth. Any project where the primary output is a body of <marked-text>interconnected knowledge</marked-text> or text.</div><div><br></div><div>Structure grows organically - start with what you know and link outward as the world expands.</div><div><br></div><div><a href="authoring/index.page">Read more</a></div></image--text--area></image--text--block>
<image--text--block data--page--block="" src="/icons/chapter-image.svg" fit="contain" layout="image-left" size="small"><image--text--area data--page--area="" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" style="outline: none;"><h2>Research</h2><div>A workspace for structured user and product research. Covers the full cycle: <marked-text>interview questionnaires</marked-text>, participant notes, <marked-text>affinity clustering</marked-text>, <marked-text>feature rating</marked-text>, and <marked-text>insight synthesis</marked-text>.</div><div><br></div><div>Good for: UX research, design thinking sprints, market research, academic studies. Anything that starts with talking to people and ends with actionable findings.</div><div><br></div><div>Boards map clusters to insights. Participant pages keep raw data traceable back to the source. Prototype logs track what was tested and what feedback it produced.</div><div><br></div><div><a href="research/index.page">Read more</a></div></image--text--area></image--text--block>
</page--root>

